-- 1. THAI FOOD & STREET FOOD GUIDE
-- ============================================================
INSERT INTO travel_guides (slug, title, subtitle, content, excerpt, category, province, image_url, image_alt, reading_time_min, tags) VALUES
(
  'thai-food-guide',
  'Thai Food & Street Food Guide: What to Eat and Where',
  'Essential dishes by region, ordering tips, spice levels, food safety, vegetarian options, and how to eat like a local on 100 THB a day',
  E'## Why Thai Food Is Worth the Deep Dive\n\nThai cuisine is not a monolith. What you eat in a Bangkok food court, a Chiang Mai night bazaar, a Krabi beachside shack, and a Ubon Ratchathani morning market are four entirely different culinary traditions that happen to share a country. Understanding this changes how you eat and where you go.\n\n## The Five Flavor Principles\n\nThai cooking balances five flavors: **sweet, sour, salty, spicy, and bitter**. A great Thai dish achieves all five in harmony. Every table at a Thai restaurant has condiments for exactly this reason -- the four-jar set of fish sauce, sugar, dried chilies, and chili vinegar is called *kruang prung* and using it is expected, not rude.\n\n## The Essential Dish Categories\n\n### Curries (Gaeng)\n\n**Green Curry (Gaeng Keow Wan):** Made with fresh green chilies and Thai basil. Sweet coconut milk base, fragrant, genuinely spicy. Common proteins: chicken, eggplant, zucchini. 80-120 THB at local restaurants, 150-200 THB at tourist spots.\n\n**Red Curry (Gaeng Daeng):** Dried red chilies, coconut milk, slightly sweeter than green. More common in tourist restaurants because it is milder and more approachable. Best version includes bamboo shoots and kaffir lime leaves.\n\n**Massaman Curry (Gaeng Massaman):** Southern Muslim-influenced curry with coconut milk, potato, peanuts, cardamom, star anise. Rich, mild, sweet. One of Thailand''s most acclaimed dishes. Order with beef.\n\n**Panang Curry:** Thicker, richer, less liquid than red curry. Peanuts ground into the paste. Usually with beef or pork. 80-120 THB.\n\n**Yellow Curry (Gaeng Karee):** Indian-influenced, turmeric-heavy, mild and sweet. Very common in the south. The tourist-friendliest curry.\n\n**Southern Curry (Gaeng Tai Pla):** Fermented fish kidney paste base -- very pungent, very spicy, very southern. Not for beginners but extraordinary if you can handle it.\n\n### Stir-Fries (Pad)\n\n**Pad Thai:** Stir-fried rice noodles, egg, bean sprouts, tofu or shrimp, tamarind, fish sauce, peanuts, lime. The international ambassador of Thai food. A great version costs 50-80 THB from a street cart. A mediocre one costs 180 THB at a tourist restaurant. Go to the street carts.\n\n**Pad Krapao (Basil Stir-Fry):** Minced pork or chicken stir-fried with holy basil, chilies, fish sauce, and oyster sauce. Served over rice with a fried egg on top. This is Thailand''s most eaten lunch dish -- what Thai office workers order every single day. 60-80 THB. Ask for *kai dao* (fried egg) on top.\n\n**Pad See Ew:** Wide rice noodles stir-fried with Chinese broccoli, egg, and protein in dark soy and oyster sauce. Slightly sweet, less spicy than Pad Thai. 60-90 THB.\n\n**Pad Woon Sen:** Glass noodles stir-fried with vegetables, egg, and usually pork or chicken. Light, slightly gelatinous texture.\n\n### Soups (Tom)\n\n**Tom Yum:** The iconic hot and sour soup. Clear broth with lemongrass, galangal, kaffir lime, chilies, mushrooms. Tom Yum Goong (shrimp) is the classic version. Often made as *nam sai* (clear broth) or *nam khon* (with evaporated milk, creamier). 80-150 THB.\n\n**Tom Kha Gai:** Coconut milk soup with chicken, galangal, lemongrass, kaffir lime, mushrooms. Milder and creamier than Tom Yum. One of Thailand''s most exported recipes. 80-120 THB.\n\n**Khao Tom:** Rice porridge soup. Plain, soothing. Sold at late-night stalls and breakfast spots. The Thai comfort food.\n\n### Salads (Yum and Tam)\n\n**Som Tam (Green Papaya Salad):** Shredded unripe papaya pounded in a mortar with palm sugar, lime juice, fish sauce, dried shrimp, peanuts, and chilies. Sour, sweet, salty, spicy -- all flavors in one dish. Central Thai version is relatively mild; Isaan version is aggressively spicy and funky. 40-60 THB from street vendors.\n\n**Larb:** Minced meat salad (pork, chicken, or beef) with toasted rice powder, fish sauce, lime, shallots, mint, and dried chilies. Isaan origin. 60-100 THB.\n\n**Yum Talay:** Seafood salad -- mixed seafood, lime, fish sauce, chilies, lemongrass. 150-250 THB at seafood restaurants.\n\n### Noodles (Kuay Tiao)\n\nOrdering noodles requires three decisions: noodle type, broth/style, and protein.\n\n**Noodle types:** *Sen yai* (wide flat rice noodles), *sen lek* (thin rice noodles, most common), *bami* (egg noodles, yellow), *woon sen* (glass noodles).\n\n**Broth styles:** *Nam* (broth), *haeng* (dry, sauce on the side), *tom yum* (spicy clear broth).\n\n**Boat Noodles (Kuay Tiao Ruea):** Small bowls of intensely flavored pork or beef broth, thick with spices. Served in small portions -- order 3-5 at once. 25-35 THB per bowl. The Ayutthaya boat noodle alley near the Bang Ian market is legendary.\n\n**Khao Soi:** The north''s signature dish -- egg noodles in a rich coconut curry broth, topped with crispy fried noodles, served with preserved cabbage and shallots. Only authentic in Chiang Mai and surrounding provinces. 60-90 THB.\n\n### Rice Dishes\n\n**Khao Man Gai:** Thai Hainanese chicken rice. Poached chicken over rice cooked in chicken broth, with ginger-soy-chili dipping sauce. 50-70 THB. The real measure of a good cook.\n\n**Khao Rad Gaeng (Cafeteria Rice):** A counter with 8-12 curries and stir-fries; point at what you want spooned over rice. 50-80 THB for a generous plate.\n\n**Khao Mok Gai:** Southern Thai biryani -- fragrant rice cooked with chicken and spices, served with cucumber ajad and spiced broth. Best found in the south and in Muslim neighborhoods.\n\n### Desserts (Khanom)\n\n**Mango Sticky Rice (Khao Niao Mamuang):** Glutinous rice in sweetened coconut milk, served with ripe mango slices and salted coconut cream. Seasonal -- best March to June. 60-100 THB.\n\n**Khanom Krok:** Small coconut milk pancakes cooked in a dimpled iron pan -- crispy edges, creamy center. 20-40 THB for 6-8 pieces.\n\n**Tub Tim Krob:** Red water chestnuts in coconut milk over shaved ice. Refreshing, sweet. 30-50 THB.\n\n**Sangkhaya Fak Thong:** Coconut custard steamed inside a whole pumpkin. Cut tableside. 40-80 THB.\n\n## Street Food Practical Guide\n\n### Ordering Without Thai\n\nFive words unlock 90% of ordering:\n\n- **Pet** (spicy) / **Mai pet** (not spicy)\n- **Aroy** (delicious) -- use liberally, vendors love it\n- **Tao rai?** (how much?)\n- **Khob khun** (thank you)\n- **Mai sai...** (without...) + the ingredient\n\n### Food Safety\n\n**Generally safe:** Cooked food served hot, food fried in front of you, food cooked in boiling liquid. A busy stall with high turnover has fresh food.\n\n**Take care with:** Raw shellfish unless you know the source, pre-cut fruit at dusty roadsides without refrigeration.\n\n**Stomach adjustments:** Even with care, your gut needs a week to adjust. Bring oral rehydration salts (ORS) and loperamide. Street food rarely causes serious illness -- the culprit is usually hotel breakfast buffets sitting in the heat.\n\n### Spice Level Reality Check\n\nThai spice is real. "Thai spicy" is not "curry house spicy." It is fresh bird''s eye chilies in genuine quantity.\n\n- **Mai pet** (not spicy): No chilies at all. Safe starting point.\n- **Pet nit noi** (a little spicy): What many Westerners consider "medium."\n- **Pet** (spicy): Authentic Thai level -- hot for most non-Thai palates.\n- **Pet mak** (very spicy): Only if you know what you are doing.\n\nDishes inherently spicy regardless of ordering: Isaan food, some som tam versions, nam prik relishes, southern curries.\n\nCoolants if you over-spice: Coconut water (instant relief), rice (absorbs spice), sweet Thai milk tea. Water makes it worse by spreading the oils.\n\n## Regional Cuisines\n\n### Central Thai (Bangkok)\n\nThe most internationally familiar version. Coconut milk curries, balanced sweet-sour-salty profiles, tom yum, pad thai, khao man gai. What became the export version of Thai food.\n\n### Northern Thai (Chiang Mai)\n\nInfluenced by Burmese, Yunnan Chinese, and Lao cooking. Less coconut milk, more spice paste, more fermented and preserved flavors.\n\n**Key dishes:** Khao Soi (coconut curry noodles), Sai Oua (herbed northern sausage with lemongrass and kaffir lime -- extraordinary), Kaeng Hang Le (Burmese-influenced pork belly curry with ginger and tamarind), Nam Prik Ong (tomato-pork chili dip with fresh vegetables).\n\n**Where to eat in Chiang Mai:** Nimman Soi 7 area for modern interpretations, the Sunday Walking Street on Wualai Road for traditional northern food stalls, Warorot Market for morning market food.\n\n### Isaan (Northeast Thailand)\n\nLao-influenced, bold, funky, fermented. Sticky rice eaten with everything. Less fatty than central Thai food.\n\n**Key dishes:** Som tam in several aggressive versions, Larb (minced meat salad), Gai Yang (grilled marinated chicken), Moo Ping (grilled pork skewers -- 10-15 THB each at breakfast), Gaeng Om (pungent herb-heavy broth).\n\n**Bangkok''s Isaan scene:** Mo Chit area and Ratchadapisek Road have excellent Isaan restaurants -- cheaper and more authentic than tourist areas.\n\n### Southern Thai\n\nCoconut milk curries meet Muslim influences from Malaysia. Turmeric-heavy yellow curries, fiery dried chili pastes, seafood everywhere.\n\n**Key dishes:** Gaeng Tai Pla (fermented fish kidney curry -- extreme and extraordinary), Massaman curry (Muslim origin, milder), Khao Yam (rice salad with dried herbs and tart fruit), Roti Mataba (stuffed flatbread).\n\n**Spice warning:** Southern Thai food is the hottest in the country. Even "mild" dishes are aggressively spiced by any international standard.\n\n## Vegetarian in Thailand\n\nFish sauce and dried shrimp appear in many "vegetable" dishes. The phrase to learn: **"Jeh"** -- Thai Buddhist vegetarian. Jeh food uses no meat, no fish sauce, no seafood products.\n\nPractical phrases:\n- **"Gin jeh"** (I eat vegetarian/vegan)\n- **"Mai sai nam pla"** (no fish sauce)\n- **"Mai sai goong haeng"** (no dried shrimp)\n- **"Mai sai neua sat"** (no meat)\n\nLook for restaurants with a yellow flag displaying Thai script -- these are certified vegetarian/vegan. Chiang Mai has an excellent vegetarian scene around Nimman and near Wat Suan Dok.\n\n## Food Courts vs Street Food vs Restaurants\n\n**Street food carts:** Cheapest, freshest, often best for single-specialty dishes. One vendor, one dish, perfected. 40-80 THB.\n\n**Night markets:** Multiple vendors in one place. Good for groups with different tastes. 50-100 THB per dish. Best: Chatuchak Park area (Bangkok, Fri-Sun), Huai Khwang (Bangkok), Chiang Mai Night Bazaar, Nimman on weekends.\n\n**Shopping mall food courts:** Underrated. Air-conditioned, hygienic, enormous variety, affordable. MBK Food Court (Bangkok), Terminal 21 food hall, Maya Mall (Chiang Mai) are excellent. 80-150 THB per dish.\n\n**Local shophouse restaurants:** Plastic chairs, no English menu, rotating chalkboard specials. The real deal. 60-120 THB.\n\n**Tourist restaurants:** Convenient, English menus, inconsistent quality, 2-3x the price. Use sparingly.\n\n## Quick Price Reference\n\n| Dish | Street Cart | Local Restaurant | Tourist Restaurant |\n|------|------------|-----------------|--------------------|\n| Pad Thai | 50-70 THB | 80-120 THB | 150-220 THB |\n| Green Curry | -- | 80-120 THB | 180-250 THB |\n| Pad Krapao | 60-80 THB | 80-100 THB | 150-200 THB |\n| Som Tam | 40-60 THB | 60-90 THB | 120-180 THB |\n| Noodle soup | 50-70 THB | 70-100 THB | 130-200 THB |\n| Mango sticky rice | 60-100 THB | 80-120 THB | 150-200 THB |\n| Iced Thai tea | 25-40 THB | 35-60 THB | 60-100 THB |\n\nFor more on what to see and eat by region, explore our [attractions](/attractions) and [festivals](/festivals) pages.',
  'Your complete guide to Thai food -- essential dishes, regional variations, street food ordering, spice levels, vegetarian options, and real prices from street carts to restaurants.',
  'food',
  NULL,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  14,
  ARRAY['food', 'street-food', 'thai-cuisine', 'restaurants', 'vegetarian', 'pad-thai', 'night-market', 'regional-food']
);

